IAA/EVG labeling requires four restricted compatibility recipes:
paidf-detection-sky, paidf-captioning-sky, paidf-visual-qa-sky, and
paidf-attribute-search-sky. Each retains its exact vendor-owned NGC parent
digest, upstream user and /app/.venv service environment. It adds missing
SkyPilot prerequisites and Git for the pinned orchestration-asset checkout,
then replaces ENTRYPOINT ["main"] with argument forwarding. The original
service CLI remains /app/.venv/bin/main. NPA setup installs into a separate
owner-writable /opt/npa-venv, with PATH preserved for login shells; it does not
install dependencies into the vendor /app/.venv. These recipes add no model weights,
datasets, credentials or recorded consent. The complete inherited image layers
still require payload/license/security review; neither source inspection nor
private registry access establishes permission to redistribute gated weights.
They are excluded from NPA public publication and are not claimed built or
functionally accepted merely because their source recipes exist.
The detection wrapper additionally removes inherited W&B/torchtitan training
tools and the unused system development toolchain through package-manager
dependency handling. RF-DETR declares W&B in its optional logging extra; the
actual service CLI and pretrained CPU inference were checked after removal.
This does not establish GPU workflow acceptance or alter the pinned model-fetch
boundary.

NPA's toolRef access closure probes each exact NGC parent repository under the
operator's credential before submission. That proves pull entitlement only and
does not grant redistribution rights. Runtime pullability and bootstrap must be
proved separately against each operator-built wrapper's exact private digest.

Gated weights remain runtime fetches under the operator's own upstream identity.
Access credentials prove fetch authorization, not permission to redistribute
weights. See `skills/NOTICE-NVIDIA-COSMOS-OSS` and
`skills/NOTICE-NVIDIA-COSMOS3` for the executable component boundaries.

